Unit 2: User interaction design
===============================
2.1. UX design process and user-centered design, Mindmap
=========================================================
UX design process
=================
The UX (User Experience) Design Process is a structured, iterative methodology used to 
create digital products that are easy, intuitive, and delightful for humans to use 
(Usability Evaluation). It focuses on understanding user needs, defining problems, and 
continuously testing solutions (Usability Evaluation).

Five stages of the UX Design Process
====================================
1. Empathize (Research): Conducting interviews, field studies (Ethnography), and 
surveys to understand the target audience's pain points.

2. Define (Strategy): Synthesizing research to map out the core user problem, 
creating user personas (A persona is the public image, character, or role a 
person adopts), and outlining the Content Strategy.

3. Ideate (Structure): Brainstorming (Brainstorming is a problem-solving and ideation 
technique where individuals or groups rapidly generate a wide range of ideas or 
solutions) layout structures, organizing data maps (Information Architecture), and 
creating paper sketches.

4. Prototype (Design): Transforming sketches into interactive, wireframe screens 
inside vector tools like Figma or Sketch.

5. Test (Evaluation): Running real-world diagnostic checks (Usability Evaluation) to 
observe user struggles and refine the layout.

User-Centered Design
====================
User-Centered Design (UCD) is an iterative design framework where designers focus on 
the users and their needs at each phase of the design process.

Mindmap
=======
A mindmap is a visual diagram used to capture, structure, and organize information
hierarchically around a single central topic. Unlike traditional, linear text or 
bulleted lists, a mind map utilizes a non-linear radial layout that mirrors the 
human brain's natural associative thinking process.

Core Elements of a Mindmap
==========================
According to the principles popularized by psychologist Tony Buzan, an effective 
mind map relies on specific visual building blocks:

Central Node: The core theme, problem, or project title positioned explicitly in 
the absolute center of the page.

Main Branches: Thick lines radiating directly from the center, representing the 
primary subtopics or high-level categories.

Sub-branches: Thinner lines extending from the main branches to break down concepts
into more specific, granular details.

Keywords: Short, single-word labels or brief phrases used on branches to maximize 
conceptual flexibility and cognitive (the mental processes involved in acquiring, 
processing, storing, and using information) speed.

Visual Anchors: The deliberate use of varying colors, shapes, icons, and drawings 
to instantly differentiate themes and stimulate memory retention (the act or 
ability to keep, hold, or maintain something).

Key Benefits
============
Integrating mind mapping into your workflow offers distinct advantages backed by 
cognitive research:

Boosts Productivity: According to workplace surveys reported by Wrike, digital mind 
mapping can increase overall productivity by up to 23%.

Improves Memory: The combination of spatial layouts and visual triggers leverages 
dual-coding in the brain, improving recall by 10%.

Saves Time: Users can overview complex documents, identify project dependencies, and 
spot conceptual gaps much faster than reviewing standard linear text.

Fosters Lateral Thinking: Breaking away from rigid outlines encourages unexpected 
connections, prompting creative problem-solving and brainstorming.

Primary Use Cases
=================
Project Management: Outlining scope, departmental dependencies, and event timelines 
before execution begins.

Education & Studying: Synthesizing lecture material, identifying gaps in 
understanding, and building active recall structures.

Content Planning: Mapping out essay structures, presentation slides, or training 
curricula.

Business Collaboration: Facilitating real-time team brainstorming sessions during 
project kickoffs


2.2 User Research: Conduct user research: Interviews, Surveys, Competitive analysis,
creating user personas, user journey mapping
====================================================================================
User Research is the systematic study of target users and their requirements to add 
realistic contexts and insights to the User-Centered Design process. It uses various
qualitative and quantitative methods to uncover user problems, behaviors, motivations,
and needs.

Interview
==========
An interview in UX design is a qualitative user research method used to gather deep
insights into a target user's background, behaviors, motivations, and pain points.

Survey
======
A survey is a quantitative and qualitative user research method used to gather 
structured feedback from a large group of target users. By asking a predefined 
set of questions, surveys allow design teams to quickly measure user demographics, 
satisfaction scores, behaviors, and recurring pain points 

Competitive Analysis
====================
Competitive analysis in UX design is the strategic practice of identifying, evaluating, 
and auditing direct and indirect competitors to understand their strengths, 
weaknesses, product features, and design patterns. 

user journey mapping
====================
User Journey Mapping is the practice of creating a timeline graphic to visualize the 
step-by-step path a user takes to achieve a goal with your product.

2.3 Ideation techniques: Using mood boards, brainstorming and sketches
======================================================================

Moodboard
=========
A moodboard is a visual collage (a combination or arrangement of pictures) of images, materials, 
text, and color samples that establishes the artistic direction and emotional tone of a project. 
It acts as a bridge between abstract ideas and concrete Visual Design, allowing designers to 
communicate a specific look and feel before committing to high-fidelity (the 
accurate and faithful reproduction of something compared to its original source)  
production work.

Brainstorming
=============
Brainstorming is a creative thinking technique used by individuals or teams to 
generate a large volume of ideas and potential solutions for a specific problem. 
In fields like UI/UX design and product development, it acts as the bridge between 
gathering user research and designing actual layouts or wireframes.

Sketches
========
Sketches in UI/UX design are quick, low-fidelity (a basic, simplified 
representation of an idea) freehand drawings used to visually 
explore layout ideas, user flows, and interface concepts before moving into digital 
tools. They serve as the first tangible translation of a concept from abstract
(something that exists as an idea) thought into a physical format during the UX 
design process.


NOTE:
=====
Figma can be used for:
======================
Moodboards
Brainstorming
Sketching
Wireframing
UI Design
Prototyping

Many UX/UI designers use Figma from the early idea stage all the way to the final 
interface design.





